WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.770
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:10.210
Approximately 7 months ago, we discussed an
AI-based technique called StyleGAN2, which

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.490
could synthesize images of human faces for
us.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:18.600
As a result, none of the faces that you see
here are real, all of them were generated

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.010
by this technique.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:24.420
The quality of the images and the amount of
detail therein is truly stunning.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:30.140
We could also exert artistic control over
these outputs by combining aspects of multiple

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:31.650
faces together.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:36.569
And as the quality of these images improve
over time, we think more and more about new

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:38.829
questions to ask about them.

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:44.469
And one of those questions is, for instance,
how original are the outputs of these networks?

00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:47.769
Can these really make something truly unique?

00:00:47.769 --> 00:00:52.010
And believe it or not, this paper gives us
a fairly good answer to that.

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:56.449
One of the key ideas in this work is that
in order to change the outputs, we have to

00:00:56.449 --> 00:00:58.329
change the model itself.

00:00:58.329 --> 00:01:01.979
Now that sounds a little nebulous, so let’s
have a look at an example.

00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:07.049
First, we choose a rule that we wish to change,
in our case, this will be the towers.

00:01:07.049 --> 00:01:13.229
We can ask the algorithm to show us matches
to this concept, and indeed, it highlights

00:01:13.229 --> 00:01:19.070
the towers on the images we haven’t marked
up yet, so it indeed understands what we meant.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:31.720
Then, we highlight the tree as a goal, place
it accordingly onto the tower, and a few seconds

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:35.100
later, there we go!

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:40.950
The model has been reprogrammed such that
instead of towers, it would make trees.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:46.539
Something original has emerged here, and,
look, not only on one image, but on multiple

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:49.340
images at the same time.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.250
Now, have a look at these human faces.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:57.110
By the way, none of them are real and were
all synthesized by StyleGAN2, the method that

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:59.340
you saw at the start of this video.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.930
Some of them do not appear to be too happy
about the research progress in machine learning,

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:07.420
but I am sure that this paper can put a smile
on their faces.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:13.220
Let’s select the ones that aren’t too
happy, then copy a big smile and paste it

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:14.800
onto their faces.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.300
See if it works!

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:19.520
It does, wow!

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:25.970
Let’s flick between the before and after
images and see how well the changes are adapted

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:28.440
to each of the target faces.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.750
Truly excellent work.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:34.960
And now, on to eyebrows.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:42.080
Hold on to your papers while we choose a few
of them, and now, I hope you agree that this

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:47.030
mustache would make glorious replacement for
them.

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:50.290
And there we go.

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:51.340
Perfect!

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:56.050
And note that with this, we are violating
Betteridge's law of headlines again in this

00:02:56.050 --> 00:03:00.670
series, because the answer to our central
question is a resounding yes, these neural

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:06.830
networks can indeed create truly original
works, and what’s more, even entire datasets

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:08.730
that haven’t existed before.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:14.360
Now, at the start of the video, we noted that
instead of editing images, it edits the neural

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.430
network’s model instead.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:21.050
If you look here, we have a set of input images
created by a generator network.

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:26.200
Then, as we highlight concepts, for instance,
the watermark text here, we can look for the

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:31.030
weights that contain this information, and
rewrite the network to accommodate these user

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:34.620
requests, in this case, to remove these patterns.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:39.640
Now that they are gone, by selecting humans,
we can again, rewrite the network weights

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:48.040
to add more of them, and finally, the signature
tree trick from earlier can take place.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:53.570
The key here is that if we change one image,
then we have a new and original image, but

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:59.960
if we change the generator model itself, we
can make thousands of new images in one go.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:02.110
Or even a full dataset.

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:03.760
Loving the idea.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.330
And perhaps the trickiest part of this work
is minimizing the effect on other weights

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:11.420
while we reprogram the ones we wish to change.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:16.880
Of course, there will always be some collateral
damage, but the results, in most cases still

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:18.900
seem to remain intact.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:22.420
Make sure to have a look at the paper to see
how it’s done exactly.

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:27.360
Also, good news, the authors also provided
an online notebook where you can try this

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:28.940
technique yourself.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:33.270
If you do, make sure to read the instructions
carefully and regardless of whether you get

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:37.870
successes or failure cases, make sure to post
them in the comments section here!

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:40.730
In research, both are useful information.

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:46.480
So, after the training step has taken place,
neural networks can be rewired to make sure

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:53.450
they create truly original works, and all
this on not one image, but on a mass scale.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:05:09.460
What 
a

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:29.210
time

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:43.540
to be alive!

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:47.099
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

